just last week this  was a sports hall now it's a testing  center for students heading home for the  holidays  from the university of east london  not a pleasant process but reassuring  when you're worried about bringing the  virus back  to your older relatives it's definitely  something that has been on my mind since  people have been talking about going  home for christmas and it's just it's  made me  much more relaxed as well as my family  back at home if you  are tested and you come back negative  you're able to just mingle with your  family without having that  extra worry it allows us to feel secure  confident  and to be in the same household just  before christmas  the government has sent thousands of  lateral flow tests to universities  across the country  the little kits return results in 30  minutes and  unlike other kinds of tests they can be  processed on the spot  although they're not as accurate  all the students we've spoken to today  say they're so glad this facility exists  because it means they can go home for  christmas and relax  with their families but just because you  taste negative today  doesn't mean you'll test negative  tomorrow  is there a risk that they relax too much  based on something which  isn't 100 percent the academic running  the center  says any test is better than none the  biggest concern that's been raised with  this lateral flow testing  is sensitivity and is not as sensitive  as the pcr test so i think for the  purposes that we're using this test  to get that snapshot of the status of  a person at that point in time  pre-travel  i think it's fit for purpose let's hope  that's right  because a lot is riding on these tests  the government just announced that  they'll be used over christmas  to let care home residents see and even  hug their families it's great to see  this new guidance that's just coming out  it it  offers a ray of light i think to  hundreds of thousands of families  and older people right across the uk  that at last after a long period of  being  able to be kept apart they're going to  be able to see each other again  to support its mass testing program the  government has cited the example of  liverpool  saying testing there brought about a  remarkable decline in cases  the data suggests a different conclusion  coronavirus cases in liverpool city were  already falling when mass testing was  introduced  and they fell in other nearby areas even  though none of these boroughs  had mass testing the scale and speed of  the mass testing effort  is remarkable but the government is  taking a big bet on it  when it can't be sure of the results  roland manthal sky news
 

